X86 grant-bump-vs-waiters-x86-FENCED
(*
 * THE SHIPPED FORM ON x86-TSO. This was written as the proposed remedy for
 * `grant-bump-vs-waiters-x86.litmus`; it is now what
 * `src/shm_lease/waitword.nim` actually compiles to. One MFENCE between the value
 * bump and the load of `waiters`.
 *
 * NOT A REGRESSION BARRIER, and an earlier version of this comment called it one.
 * A `.litmus` file is a standalone model: herd7's verdict is a function of this
 * file alone, so no edit to `src/` can move it -- deleting `fullFence()` leaves
 * this Never exactly where it is. The mechanical barrier is
 * `tests/check-fence-shape.sh` (`just test-fence-shape`), which disassembles the
 * shipped `wakeAll` / `wakeOne` and requires a full-fence instruction ahead of the
 * `waiters` load. This file pins the INTENDED shape.
 *
 * In C that is `atomicThreadFence(ATOMIC_SEQ_CST)` after `publishValue` and
 * before `wakeAll` reads the waiter count -- which is EXACTLY the Dekker pairing
 * `src/shm_lease/obsring.nim` already applies to its structurally identical
 * idle-token / `tail - head` pair, "rather than relying on whatever ordering a
 * particular CAS mapping happens to emit on ARM64". The fix was adopted from the
 * pattern the library already contained; `fullFence()` in `wakeAll` / `wakeOne`
 * is the same three words.
 *
 * The C-level companion is `grant-bump-vs-waiters-FENCED-fix.litmus`; the arm64
 * one is `grant-bump-vs-waiters-aarch64-FENCED.litmus`.
 *
 * (Making the value store SEQ-CST instead would also work -- on x86 that lowers
 * to a LOCKed instruction, which is a full barrier. See
 * `grant-bump-vs-waiters-SEQCST-fix.litmus` for that form in C.)
 *
 * RESULT (herd7 7.58, 2026-08-17): **FORBIDDEN / "Never 0 3"**.
 *)
{ value=0; waiters=0; 1:EBX=1; }
 P0                 | P1                 ;
 MOV [value],$1     | XCHG [waiters],EBX ;
 MFENCE             | MOV EAX,[value]    ;
 MOV EAX,[waiters]  |                    ;
exists (0:EAX=0 /\ 1:EAX=0)
